1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,200
When you refer to values or goals that are shared, or that the other person knows you

2
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:10,040
support, you're showing the other person the benefits to him or her that they genuinely

3
00:00:10,040 --> 00:00:13,360
believe would result from taking the action.

4
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:18,360
This is not the same as offering incentives that you control, which is part of the negotiation

5
00:00:18,360 --> 00:00:19,360
tactic.

6
00:00:19,360 --> 00:00:23,760
It is based in your understanding of what's important to the other and your ability to

7
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:27,720
make the connection between the action you're asking the other to take and something that

8
00:00:27,720 --> 00:00:30,560
he or she believes in or hopes for.

9
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:36,519
It's a more personal form of persuasion, but the logical connection must still be obvious.

10
00:00:36,519 --> 00:00:37,519
Here are some examples.

11
00:00:37,519 --> 00:00:43,780
I know that you're focused on developing your team leadership skills right now.

12
00:00:43,780 --> 00:00:47,960
This opportunity will give you a chance to put the skills to use and get some practical

13
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:48,960
experience.

14
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:52,760
We're both committed to getting support for this proposal.

15
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:56,960
I think if you'll do this, there's a good chance we can convince senior management to

16
00:00:56,960 --> 00:00:58,239
fund it.

17
00:00:58,239 --> 00:01:02,199
I see this as a way for you to make progress on your initiative.

